novo. Carlson v. Glob. Client Sols., LLC, 171 Wn.2d 486, 493, 256 P.3d 321 (2011). We also review the meaning of a statute de novo. Dep't of Ecology v. Campbell & Gwinn, LLC, 146 Wn.2d 1, 9, 43 P.3d 4 (2003). We have long held that pro se litigants are bound by the same rules of procedure and substantive law as licensed attorneys. Holder v. City of Vancouver, 136 Wn. App. 104, 106, 147 P.3d 641 (2006); In re Marriage of Olson, 69 Wn. App. 621, 626, 850 P.2d 527 (1993) (noting courts are under no obligation to grant special favors to . . . a pro se litigant.). Thus, a pro se appellant's failure to identify any specific legal issues . . . cite any authority or comply with procedural rules may still preclude appellate review. State v. Marintorres, 93 Wn. App. 442, 452, 969 P.2d 501 (1999). Lopez Demetrio v. Sakuma Bros. Farms, 183 Wn.2d 649, 355 P.3d 258 (2015). Spokane County v. Dep't of Fish & Wildlife, 192 Wn.2d 453, 430 P.3d 655 (2018)."}
